# Security Rules

## Private listing fields — never in public responses

The following Listing fields are **private** and must never appear in a public API response:

| Field | Why private |
|-------|-------------|
| `addressFull` | Exact address — revealed only after unlock payment |
| `mapFull` | Full map — revealed only after unlock payment |
| `landlordName` | Contact info — revealed only after unlock payment |
| `landlordPhone` | Contact info — revealed only after unlock payment |

Always use the service-layer mapper functions that strip these fields:

```typescript
// Correct — toPublicListing strips private fields
return toPublicListing(listing);

// Also correct — model instance method
return listing.toPublicObject();

// Wrong — raw document exposed directly
return successResponse({ listing: listing.toObject() });
```

When returning unlocked data (user has an approved PaymentProof for the listing), use `getUnlockedListing()` or `toUnlockedListing()` — these deliberately include the private fields but must only be called after verifying unlock status.

```typescript
const isUnlocked = user.unlockedListingIds.some(id => id.toString() === listingId);
const listingData = isUnlocked
  ? await listingsService.getUnlockedListing(listingId)
  : await listingsService.getListingById(listingId);
```

## Password field — select: false

The `password` field on `User` is marked `select: false` in the Mongoose schema. This means a standard `User.findOne()` call will NOT include the password.

To compare passwords (login), always use:

```typescript
const user = await User.findByEmail(email); // adds +password selector
await user.comparePassword(candidatePassword);
```

Never do:

```typescript
const user = await User.findOne({ email }); // password field missing — comparison will fail
```

Never include `password` in any API response shape.

## Email enumeration prevention

Login and forgot-password endpoints must return the same generic message whether or not the email exists, to prevent attackers from enumerating valid accounts:

```typescript
// Correct
return errorResponse('Invalid email or password', 401);

// Wrong — reveals whether email exists
if (!user) return errorResponse('No account found with this email', 401);
```

The forgot-password route always returns success-looking message even when no account matches:

```typescript
return successResponse({ message: 'If an account with this email exists, a reset link has been sent.' });
```

## Zod validation before DB

Every API route that accepts user input must validate it with Zod `safeParse` **before** any database operation. No unvalidated data touches MongoDB.

```typescript
const validation = schema.safeParse(body);
if (!validation.success) return validationErrorResponse(validation.error); // stop here
await connectDB();
await SomeModel.create(validation.data); // only validated data reaches DB
```

## Phone number format

All phone numbers stored in the system must match the Nigerian international format:

```
/^\+234\d{10}$/
```

This regex is enforced in:
- `UserSchema` (`phone` field)
- `ListingSchema` (`landlordPhone` field)
- `src/lib/validators/auth.server.ts` (registration)

Never accept local formats like `080XXXXXXXX` or `+2348XXXXXXXXX` — the regex is strict.

## JWT secret strength

In production, `JWT_SECRET` must be at least 32 characters. This is enforced at startup in `src/lib/config/env.ts`:

```typescript
if (isProduction && jwtSecret.length < 32) {
  throw new Error('JWT_SECRET must be at least 32 characters in production');
}
```

Never hardcode a JWT secret in code. Always read from `process.env.JWT_SECRET`.

## Security headers

`src/middleware.ts` sets security headers on every non-API response:

```
X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
X-Frame-Options: DENY
X-XSS-Protection: 1; mode=block
Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
```

Do not remove these. If adding new middleware behaviour, preserve this header block.

## Production error exposure

`src/lib/api/response.ts` uses `isProduction` to strip internal error details from responses. The `serverErrorResponse()` helper always returns a generic "An unexpected error occurred" message to clients in production, regardless of the actual error.

Never add code that returns raw `error.message` or stack traces in production responses.

## Admin-only endpoints

The `/api/docs` and `/api/health/email` routes return 404/403 in production. Do not make these accessible in production:

```typescript
if (isProduction) {
  return NextResponse.json({ error: 'Not found' }, { status: 404 });
}
```

## Cloudinary images — wildcard hostname

`next.config.ts` uses `hostname: '**'` in `remotePatterns`, allowing any HTTPS source for `next/image`. This is intentional for Cloudinary CDN URLs but means all HTTPS image URLs are trusted. Do not change this without understanding the impact on uploaded media.

